{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjs5Cu4GHYcoGCVg6q6v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AHC8iVQEPzFK3K8Jupzqvv","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/START_HERE.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2773,"cache_read_input_tokens":54638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2773},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"9040433d-5e42-4bde-bf1d-79baea7d990f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:30.357Z","request_id":"req_011Cgjs5CbCMcNeHWAiKL63B","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01AHC8iVQEPzFK3K8Jupzqvv":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/START_HERE.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AHC8iVQEPzFK3K8Jupzqvv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n2\t\n3\tA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\n4\tand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\n5\thttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\n6\tcaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n7\t\n8\t## Which route\n9\t- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n10\t  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n11\t- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n12\t  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n13\t  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n14\t\n15\tPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n16\t\n17\t> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n18\t> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n19\t> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n20\t> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n21\t> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n22\t\n23\t> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n24\t\n25\tAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\n26\tsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n27\t\n28\t## Linux setup\n29\tUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\n30\tRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\n31\ttoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n32\t\n33\tOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\n34\talready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\n35\tto install missing packages.\n36\t\n37\t```sh\n38\tsudo apt-get update\n39\tsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n40\t  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n41\t  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\tSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\n45\tMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\n46\tand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n47\t\n48\t## Build and inspect headlessly\n49\t\n50\t```sh\n51\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n52\t./target/debug/pav help\n53\t./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n54\t./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\t`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\n58\ttown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\n59\tkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n60\t\n61\t```sh\n62\tprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n63\t./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n64\t./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\n65\tcargo test --locked -p pav_core\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\n69\tevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\n70\tbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\n71\twait for a server.\n72\t\n73\t## Run the game window\n74\t\n75\t```sh\n76\tCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n77\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n78\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n79\t./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n80\t```\n81\t\n82\tWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\n83\theadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\n84\tsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n85\t\n86\tFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\n87\tadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n88\t\n89\t## The studios (live editing in the window)\n90\t- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n91\t  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n92\t  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n93\t- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n94\t  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n95\t- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n96\t  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n97\t\n98\tEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\n99\tseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n100\t\n101\t## Make another handoff archive\n102\tFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\n103\tinto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\n104\tfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\n105\tunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\n106\twould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n107\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"9da2a9d2-6728-4b9a-be39-b6542dacbd3f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:30.367Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/START_HERE.md","content":"# Shardfall and the Pavilion engine: start here\n\nA Rust game engine built for AI agents (Pavilion), the hack-and-slash built on it (Shardfall)\nand a pavilion of demo rooms. This page is for an agent receiving the source, from a clone of\nhttps://github.com/michaelcrosato/shardfall or from a handoff archive (no Git history, build\ncaches, saved heroes or executables; `git init` if you want to track your changes).\n\n## Which route\n- **Make a new game on the engine:** read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md` whole. It is the only page\n  you need to start; it begins from the starter game (`crates/pav_core/src/games/`).\n- **Work on the engine or on Shardfall:** read `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, `AGENTS.md` and\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md` (Claude Code loads them through `CLAUDE.md`); `docs/DESIGN.md` and\n  `docs/GAME.md` when the work touches the design.\n\nPrompts to give the receiving agent:\n\n> Make a game: read START_HERE.md and docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md, install the Linux packages and\n> build the `pav` CLI. Then make *(your idea, e.g. \"a top-down maze where you collect five\n> keys and escape while ghosts patrol\")* on the engine, starting from the starter game. Give\n> it a bot that wins and the tests from the guide, check it with the capture tools, tell me\n> how to play it, and end with the engine feedback the guide asks for.\n\n> Work on the engine: read START_HERE.md, then follow AGENTS.md. *(the task)*\n\nAgents in auto modes may refuse to unpack an archive or run its scripts unless the prompt\nsays to; say so in the prompt when you hand over an archive.\n\n## Linux setup\nUse a Linux machine with Rust installed through rustup. `rust-toolchain.toml` selects\nRust 1.98.1 automatically. The first build needs network access to download the\ntoolchain and dependencies; `Cargo.lock` pins the dependency versions.\n\nOn Debian / Ubuntu, install the native build and runtime dependencies below. If\nalready running as root, omit `sudo`. On a managed machine, ask its administrator\nto install missing packages.\n\n```sh\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install -y build-essential pkg-config libasound2-dev libudev-dev \\\n  mesa-vulkan-drivers vulkan-tools xvfb libxkbcommon-x11-0 libx11-xcb1 \\\n  libxcursor1 libxrandr2 libxi6\n```\n\nSimulation tools need no display or GPU. Screenshot tools use Vulkan and can use\nMesa lavapipe (software rendering) on a machine without a GPU. Keep builds on disk\nand limit parallel jobs on machines with little RAM.\n\n## Build and inspect headlessly\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_tools --bin pav\n./target/debug/pav help\n./target/debug/pav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=5\n./target/debug/pav capture scene=world ticks=60 width=640 height=360 out=out/pavilion.png\n```\n\n`starter` is the starter game; `world` is the pavilion of demo rooms; `town` is Shardfall's\ntown; `arena` is the wave arena; `lab` is the Menagerie; `level/1` starts the descent. A room\nkey such as `drift` loads that demo by itself. `pav repl` keeps a session across commands:\n\n```sh\nprintf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=drift\\ncapture width=640 height=360 out=out/drift.png\\n' | ./target/debug/pav repl\n./target/debug/pav levelmap depth=7 nav=true out=out/level7.png\n./target/debug/pav campaign scene=town from=1 to=3 wall=60\ncargo test --locked -p pav_core\n```\n\nInspect the generated images with the agent's image viewer. `docs/REFERENCE.md` describes\nevery tool. `.mcp.json` exposes the same CLI as the `shardfall` MCP server; run the `cargo\nbuild` above before starting an MCP client, because a first build takes longer than clients\nwait for a server.\n\n## Run the game window\n\n```sh\nCARGO_BUILD_JOBS=4 cargo build --locked -p pav_app\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene starter # the starter game\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene world   # tech demo\n./target/debug/shardfall --scene town    # Shardfall\n```\n\nWithout a graphical desktop, use `xvfb-run -a` around the game command, or use the\nheadless capture tools above. The demo's F2 menu jumps between rooms. Startup\nsettings and hero saves are written next to the game executable.\n\nFor Windows cross-compilation and Wine checks, `scripts/setup-linux.sh` installs the\nadditional tools and `scripts/build-windows.sh` builds the executables.\n\n## The studios (live editing in the window)\n- **Objects:** `scripts/asset-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.studio.json` for the LLM: `assets`,\n  `asset_edit`, `asset_preview`, `asset_spawn` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`,\n  `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`).\n- **Animation:** `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, with `.mcp.animation-studio.json` (or `pav\n  live` in a second terminal): `anim_edit`, `anim_preview` (`docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`).\n- **Creatures:** Node 22.18 or newer, then `scripts/creature-studio.sh`; `creature_edit`\n  returns a job to follow with `creature_status` (`docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`).\n\nEdits save to readable files and show in the window without a rebuild. `.mcp.json` runs a\nseparate headless session; the studio configurations control the visible window.\n\n## Make another handoff archive\nFrom a Git checkout, `scripts/package-agent.sh` packages the tracked files (with local edits)\ninto `out/agent/shardfall-linux-agent.tar.gz` and a SHA-256 checksum; add new files to Git\nfirst. It leaves out `pavilion-lite/` (an older engine, frozen). `scripts/check-handoff.sh`\nunpacks a fresh archive elsewhere, builds it and plays the starter, as a receiving agent\nwould: run it after changing what the archive carries or this page.\n","numLines":107,"startLine":1,"totalLines":107}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01AHC8iVQEPzFK3K8Jupzqvv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjs5Cu4GHYcoGCVg6q6v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015Uc9EJhsLkA94YUeh4vdKT","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2773,"cache_read_input_tokens":54638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2773},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"617f9aff-392a-42e2-aa2d-3152eed74708","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:31.504Z","request_id":"req_011Cgjs5CbCMcNeHWAiKL63B","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_015Uc9EJhsLkA94YUeh4vdKT":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Uc9EJhsLkA94YUeh4vdKT","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Make a game on Pavilion\n2\t\n3\tFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\n4\tis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\n5\twas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\n6\tcommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n7\t\n8\t## What you get\n9\t- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n10\t  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n11\t  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n12\t  and replays are exact.\n13\t- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n14\t  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n15\t- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n16\t- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n17\t  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n18\t- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n19\t  one self-contained HTML file).\n20\t\n21\tShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\n22\tare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n23\t**starter game**.\n24\t\n25\t## Read this, skip that\n26\t- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n27\t  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n28\t  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n29\t- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n30\t  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n31\t  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n32\t  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n33\t- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n34\t  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n35\t  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n36\t\n37\t## Quick start\n38\t```sh\n39\tcargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\n40\talias pav=./target/debug/pav\n41\tpav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\n42\tpav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\n43\tprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\n44\tcargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\n45\tcargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n46\t```\n47\t`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\n48\tsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n49\t\n50\t**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n51\t`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\n52\tA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\n53\tagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n54\t\n55\t## Your game in six steps\n56\t1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n57\t   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n58\t   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n59\t2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n60\t   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n61\t   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n62\t3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n63\t   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n64\t4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n65\t5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n66\t   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n67\t6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n68\t   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n69\t\n70\t## The level: a room file\n71\tA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n72\t`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\n73\tcorridor. In a room file:\n74\t- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n75\t  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n76\t  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n77\t  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n78\t  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n79\t- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n80\t  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n81\t- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n82\t  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n83\t  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n84\t- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n85\t  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n86\t- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n87\t  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n88\t  the level: `pav params`).\n89\t\n90\t- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n91\t- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n92\t\n93\tRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\n94\tfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\n95\tfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n96\t`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n97\t\n98\t## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n99\t```rust\n100\t#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\n101\tpub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n102\t\n103\timpl Game for MyGame {\n104\t    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n105\t    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n106\t        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n107\t    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n108\t    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n109\t    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n110\t    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n111\t    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n112\t}\n113\tpub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n114\t```\n115\tEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\n116\tnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\n117\ttheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\n118\tof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\n119\talready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\n120\tcharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\n121\tyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n122\t`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n123\t\n124\t## Building blocks\n125\t| You need | Use |\n126\t|---|---|\n127\t| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n128\t| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n129\t| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n130\t| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n131\t| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n132\t| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n133\t| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n134\t| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n135\t| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n136\t| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n137\t| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n138\t| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n139\t\n140\t## Rules that keep it working\n141\t- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n142\t  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n143\t- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n144\t  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n145\t  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n146\t- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n147\t  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n148\t- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n149\t  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n150\t\n151\t## Check your work (no screen needed)\n152\t| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n153\t|---|---|\n154\t| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n155\t| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n156\t| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n157\t| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n158\t| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n159\t| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n160\t| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n161\t| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n162\t\n163\t## Ship it\n164\t- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n165\t  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n166\t- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n167\t- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n168\t  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n169\t\n170\t## Limits today\n171\t- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n172\t  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n173\t- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n174\t  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n175\t- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n176\t- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n177\t  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n178\t- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n179\t\n180\t## Report back\n181\tWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\n182\tagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\n183\twhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\n184\tand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n185\t`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n186\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"7bdcced8-8010-4276-95b1-cf0baa12efa7","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:31.518Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md","content":"# Make a game on Pavilion\n\nFor an agent asked to build a game on this engine. Read this page whole (about 3k tokens); it\nis the only one you need to start. Search the others when a step points to them. The engine\nwas made for agents: everything runs, renders and checks headless, and every tool is a CLI\ncommand, a REPL line and an MCP tool.\n\n## What you get\n- **A simulation** on the CPU at 60 ticks a second (`pav_core`): characters that walk, jump,\n  climb and swim, rapier physics, pathfinding, projectiles, zones, procedural animation,\n  props from a library. The same inputs and seed always play the same: snapshots, rewind\n  and replays are exact.\n- **A WebGPU renderer** (`pav_render`, `pav_view`): lighting, shadows, bloom, styles and\n  filters (cel, pixel, painterly...), set per level as data.\n- **Synthesized sound** (`pav_audio`): it plays the simulation's events, no audio files.\n- **Tools** (`pav`): capture a frame, step time, drive the player, run your game's bot over\n  many seeds, record and replay. Images come back as PNGs you can look at.\n- **Three builds** from one source: a window on Linux and Windows, and the browser (also as\n  one self-contained HTML file).\n\nShardfall, the hack-and-slash in `crates/pav_core/src/arpg/`, and the pavilion of demo rooms\nare built on the same engine. They are examples, not starting points: start from the\n**starter game**.\n\n## Read this, skip that\n- **Use:** this page; `crates/pav_core/src/games/` (the hook and the starter, about 600\n  lines); `rooms/_template.toml` (the level format: search it for a field); `docs/REFERENCE.md`\n  \"Tools\" (search for a tool); `pav help`.\n- **`AGENTS.md`** is the rulebook for agents working on the engine and Shardfall. Its\n  \"Key rules\" apply to your game too (below). Its workflow does not: `docs/HISTORY.md`,\n  `docs/PROGRESS.md`, pull requests, escalations and the asset-store plans are the\n  maintainers'. Follow your own task's instructions for commits and delivery.\n- **Skip:** `docs/HISTORY.md`, `docs/ASSET_SYSTEM_PLAN.md`, `docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`,\n  Shardfall's code and data (`arpg/`, `game/`), `pavilion-lite/` (an older, frozen engine).\n  If you need to read engine source to use the engine, say so in your feedback (below).\n\n## Quick start\n```sh\ncargo build -p pav_tools --bin pav                     # first build: a few minutes\nalias pav=./target/debug/pav\npav play scene=starter bot=true seeds=10               # the starter's bot wins 10/10\npav capture scene=starter ticks=120 width=640 height=360 out=out/starter.png\nprintf 'load scene=starter\\ninput move=[1,0] ticks=60\\nplay\\ncapture out=out/a.png\\n' | pav repl\ncargo test -p pav_core --test games                    # the starter's tests\ncargo run -p pav_app -- --scene starter                # the window (WASD; needs a display)\n```\n`START_HERE.md` has the Linux packages to install. Look at captures with your image tool;\nsmall sizes are cheaper. With MCP (`.mcp.json`), the same tools are `mcp__shardfall__*`.\n\n**The edit loop.** Running from the repository's root, `pav` reads your level file at every\n`load` (and `play seeds=`), so changes to the map and to its `[game]` numbers need no rebuild.\nA change to Rust code rebuilds `pav` (about 2 minutes on 4 cores) and a test binary (as long\nagain): batch code edits, and put balance numbers in `[game]`.\n\n## Your game in six steps\n1. **Copy the starter.** `crates/pav_core/src/games/starter.rs` and `starter.toml` to\n   `<key>.rs` and `<key>.toml` in the same folder. Rename the struct; set `const KEY` to your\n   key (lowercase, no `/`); point `include_str!` at your level.\n2. **Register it.** In `games/mod.rs`: `pub mod <key>;` and one line in `GAMES`:\n   `entry::<mykey::MyGame>(\"One sentence about it.\", mykey::build)`. Now `load scene=<key>`\n   works in the tools, the window (`--scene <key>`) and the browser (`?scene=<key>`).\n3. **Draw the level** in `<key>.toml` (next section), with your rules' numbers in its `[game]`\n   table (the starter's `Rules`). `pav room_check path=<file>` checks it.\n4. **Write the rules** in your `Game` impl (the hook, below).\n5. **Teach the bot** every rule you add (`Game::bot`): it is how you, the tests and the next\n   agent find out whether the game can be won.\n6. **Copy the tests** in `crates/pav_core/tests/games.rs` to `tests/<key>.rs`: the bot wins on\n   ten seeds, a careless player loses, and snapshots, rewind and replays are exact.\n\n## The level: a room file\nA level is a room file (`rooms/_template.toml` documents every field; the pavilion's\n`rooms/*.toml` are working examples). Rooms built this way stand alone, with no wing or\ncorridor. In a room file:\n- `[layout]`: an ASCII map per floor (`[[layout.layer]]`, 1 cell = 1 m; column = x east,\n  row = z south) and a `legend` per character: floor and wall `blocks` (heights, colours,\n  `destructible`, `crumble`, `ghost`), ladders, `prop`s, `label`s, `grass`, trigger `zone`s\n  (`kill`, `water`, `checkpoint`, `conveyor`, `bounce`, `pad`, `camera`, course\n  `start`/`gate`/`finish`) and `marker = \"<name>\"`.\n- **Markers** are your own pieces: `crate::scenes::markers(sim, \"shard\")` lists every\n  marked cell (world positions, map order). `marker = \"player\"` is the start.\n- `[[npc]]`: characters (`kind = biped | spider | lizard | beetle | blob | quadruped`, `look` colours and\n  proportions, `ai = idle | wander | patrol | circle | follow | guard`). Your game can drive\n  them instead (the starter's slimes).\n- `[[object]]`: library props (`asset = \"LIB/core/<name>\"`; `pav assets find=barrel` searches\n  the store, `assets shelf=prop` lists the best), physics objects, `[[joint]]`s and `[[chain]]`s.\n- `[camera]` (`tilt`, `distance`, `yaw`, `ortho`), `[view]` (light, sky, fog, bloom, styles,\n  filters: `pav params prefix=view` lists them), `[params]` (movement and physics while in\n  the level: `pav params`).\n\n- `[game]`: your rules' numbers, free-form; your `build` reads them (`def.game`).\n- `entrance` is required by the format; with a `player` marker a level doesn't use it.\n\nRead the level with `games::level_text(KEY, include_str!(...))`: the file on disk when running\nfrom the repository, else the copy built in (tests, the browser, an executable). The camera\nfollows the player; to see a whole level from above, `camera preset=top` then\n`set path=camera.distance value=30` (raise it for bigger levels) and `capture`.\n\n## The hook: `pav_core::games::Game`\n```rust\n#[derive(Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct MyGame { /* the rules' state: score, timers, entity ids, outcome */ }\n\nimpl Game for MyGame {\n    const KEY: &'static str = \"mygame\";\n    fn pre(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, input: &InputFrame, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>)\n        -> BTreeMap<EntityId, InputFrame>;          // before movement: inputs for characters you drive\n    fn post(&mut self, sim: &mut Sim, dt: f32, events: &mut Vec<SimEvent>); // after physics: rules\n    fn hud(&self, sim: &Sim) -> Vec<String>;        // text lines, top left; `play` returns them\n    fn status(&self, sim: &Sim) -> serde_json::Value; // your state for tools and tests\n    fn outcome(&self) -> Outcome;                   // Playing | Won | Lost\n    fn bot(&self, sim: &Sim) -> InputFrame;         // what a good player would press now\n}\npub fn build(sim: &mut Sim, rest: Option<&str>) -> Result<()>; // the scene; sets sim.state.play\n```\nEvery method has a default. `build` gets the rest of a scene name `mygame/<rest>` (a level\nnumber, say). Each tick runs: behaviours, your `pre`, characters move (your inputs replace\ntheir own), physics, shots move and hit, your `post`. While `pre`/`post` run, your game is out\nof `sim.state.play`: use `self` for your state and `sim` for the world. In `post`, `events`\nalready holds this tick's events: read them to react (`Hit { target, .. }` names the\ncharacter a shot struck: it is stunned, knocked back and safe from shots for 0.6 s), and push\nyour own. From outside, `sim.play()?.get::<MyGame>()` reads your game; in a test,\n`sim.state.play.as_mut().unwrap().get_mut::<MyGame>().unwrap()` changes it.\n\n## Building blocks\n| You need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| The player | `sim.state.player`; it walks by the keys' `InputFrame` (`move_dir`, `buttons::JUMP`, `PRIMARY`...); `sim.feet(id)` is where a character stands (`pos` is its centre) |\n| Characters | `[[npc]]` in the level, or `sim.spawn_npc(name, feet, facing, PuppetDef::preset(BodyPlan::Blob), None, None)`; drive them from `pre` |\n| Pickups, hazards, scenery | `sim.spawn(Spawn::new(\"name\", pos).visual(Visual::new(shape, Color::hex(\"#7fe3ff\"))).behavior(Behavior::Spin { speed }))`; `sim.despawn(id)`; `sim.set_position(id, feet)` |\n| Moving platforms, emitters, spinners | `Behavior::Move`, `Rotate`, `Emitter`, `Spawner` (`entity.rs`), or `[[object]]` in the level |\n| Shots | `sim.state.projectiles.spawn(Projectile { pos, vel, radius, life, color, knockback, gravity, owner, team, damage })`: plain structs, stopped by walls; `Team::Enemy` shots hit characters (a `Hit` event), `Team::Player` shots hit things with `health` |\n| Pathfinding | `nav::NavGrid::for_room(sim)` (the level's walkable grid); `grid.path(from, to)`; `grid.flow(goal, reach)` steers many chasers at once; `grid.walkable(p)` |\n| Randomness | `sim.state.rng` only (`range`, `below`, `f32`): seeded, so a seed always plays the same |\n| Messages | `sim.say(\"text\")`: centre top for a few seconds |\n| Sound and effects | push `SimEvent`s from `pre`/`post`: `Coin`, `Pickup`, `Hit`, `Explosion`, `Shot`, `Jump`, `Land`... The renderer and the synth play them |\n| Tuning | your numbers: the level's `[game]`; the engine's: `pav params`, `pav set path=movement.speed value=4.5` (movement, physics, camera, view), and a level's `[params]` set them while in it |\n| Looks | the level's `[view]`; `pav look` lists looks and filters; `rooms/mix_match.toml` mixes styles per part |\n| Animation | characters animate procedurally; motion clips: `pav assets shelf=clip`, then `clips = [\"SET/Clip\"]` on an `[[npc]]` |\n\n## Rules that keep it working\n- **Same inputs and seed, same game.** Random numbers come from `sim.state.rng`; never read the\n  clock; never let `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (`BTreeMap`, or sort).\n- **All game state lives in your struct or in entities**, and your struct is `Clone` + serde\n  (snapshots are clones; files and the replay hash are its CBOR). A cache that follows from\n  the level (a nav grid) is `#[serde(skip)]` and rebuilt when missing.\n- **`pav_core` has no window, GPU or audio code**, and game code must run in the browser: no\n  threads, blocking calls or file access (embed data with `include_str!`).\n- **Check with the tools, not by reading engine code.** If a tool or error message didn't tell\n  you what you needed, that is engine feedback.\n\n## Check your work (no screen needed)\n| Question | Command (`pav <tool>` or a `pav repl` line) |\n|---|---|\n| Can it be won? Lost? | `play bot=true seeds=10` (wins, seconds, the state of every run not won) |\n| What's on screen? | `capture width=640 height=360 out=out/a.png`; `filmstrip` for motion in one image; to watch the bot, repeat `play bot=true ticks=30` and `capture` in a `pav repl` |\n| Does a rule fire? | `input move=[1,0] ticks=30`, then `play` (HUD, outcome, `status`) |\n| Where is everything? | `entities`, `player`, `status` (with the state hash) |\n| Same every time? | `record_save path=out/r.json`, then `replay path=out/r.json` (checks the hash); `rewind`, `snapshot_save`/`snapshot_load` |\n| Is the level valid? | `room_check path=crates/pav_core/src/games/<key>.toml` |\n| Does it run fast? | `bench scene=<key> ticks=1200` |\n| Does it still pass? | `cargo test -p pav_core --test <key>`, `cargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo fmt --all` |\n\n## Ship it\n- **Window:** `cargo run -p pav_app -- --scene <key>` (Linux; `xvfb-run -a` without a display).\n  F5 restarts the game (Esc opens the menu).\n- **Windows `.exe`:** `scripts/build-windows.sh` (after `scripts/setup-linux.sh`).\n- **Browser, one file:** `scripts/build-web.sh --single-file --scene <key> --title \"My Game\"\n  --out out/mygame.html`: about 10 MB, runs from `file://` in Chrome or Edge (WebGPU).\n\n## Limits today\n- HUD: text lines only (captures draw them; `ui` reads them). Panels, bars or menus mean egui\n  code in `pav_ui` (draw it from `headless.rs` too, so the tools see it).\n- Drawing from game state without an entity (`pav_view::assets::emit_asset`) and new sounds\n  (`pav_audio`, a closed list) mean code outside `pav_core`.\n- Your game compiles into the engine crate (`pav_core/src/games/`), beside Shardfall.\n- The camera follows the player with the level's `[camera]` settings; there is no\n  per-game camera code. Keep levels about 20 m across, or raise `[camera] distance`.\n- The window's controls card lists the engine demo's keys (bombs, vehicles), not your game's.\n\n## Report back\nWhen you hand the game back, add a short **engine feedback** section, written for an engine\nagent who has never seen your work: what you used, the engine source you had to read and\nwhy, what you wrote that the engine should have had (with the file), wrong or missing docs,\nand the one change that would have helped most. The maintainers log it in\n`docs/FEEDBACK.md`, and what keeps coming back becomes engine work.\n","numLines":186,"startLine":1,"totalLines":186}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_015Uc9EJhsLkA94YUeh4vdKT","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgjs5Cu4GHYcoGCVg6q6v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01BcKoEj2odSHGzW9JBTQkEF","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/AGENTS.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2773,"cache_read_input_tokens":54638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2773},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"bf08156b-bcd2-4a45-8a98-6b65495bdad0","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:32.661Z","request_id":"req_011Cgjs5CbCMcNeHWAiKL63B","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01BcKoEj2odSHGzW9JBTQkEF":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/AGENTS.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BcKoEj2odSHGzW9JBTQkEF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n2\t\n3\tMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\n4\tyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n5\t\n6\tShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\n7\tprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n8\t`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\n9\tthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\n10\tread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\n11\ttool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\n12\tneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\n13\tread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\n14\tCLI examples.\n15\t\n16\t## How the doctrine applies here\n17\tAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\n18\tfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n19\t- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n20\t  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n21\t  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n22\t  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n23\t- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n24\t  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n25\t  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n26\t- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n27\t  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n28\t  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n29\t  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n30\t  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n31\t  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n32\t  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n33\t- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n34\t  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n35\t- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n36\t  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n37\t  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n38\t  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n39\t  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n40\t  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n41\t  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n42\t- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n43\t  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n44\t  log it and continue.\n45\t- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n46\t  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n47\t\n48\t## Working rules\n49\tUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n50\t1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n51\t2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n52\t3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n53\t   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n54\t4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n55\t5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n56\t   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n57\t6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n58\t\n59\t## Testing budget (prototype stage)\n60\tEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\n61\tmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\n62\trecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n63\t- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n64\t  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n65\t  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n66\t- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n67\t  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n68\t  errors.\n69\t- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n70\t  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n71\t  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n72\t  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n73\t- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n74\t  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n75\t  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n76\t  are noise.\n77\t- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n78\t  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n79\t  the user on a quick comparison page.\n80\t- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n81\t  a verified benefit.\n82\t- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n83\t  whole context.\n84\t- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n85\t  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n86\t  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n87\t- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n88\t  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n89\t    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n90\t  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n91\t  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n92\t    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n93\t  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n94\t    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n95\t\n96\t## Architecture\n97\tCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n98\t- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n99\t  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n100\t  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n101\t- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n102\t  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n103\t- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n104\t- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n105\t- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n106\t  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n107\t- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n108\t  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n109\t- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n110\t  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n111\t\n112\tData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n113\t`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\n114\tmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\n115\tData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n116\t-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n117\t\n118\tKey rules:\n119\t- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n120\t- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n121\t- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n122\t  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n123\t  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n124\t- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n125\t  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n126\t- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n127\t- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n128\t  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n129\t\n130\t## Build, run, test\n131\t```sh\n132\tscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\n133\tcargo build                        # everything (Linux)\n134\tcargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\n135\tcargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\n136\tcargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\n137\tcargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\n138\tscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\n139\tscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\n140\tscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\n141\tscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\n142\tscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\n143\tscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n144\t```\n145\t`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\n146\tother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\n147\tBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\n148\tsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\n149\tbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\n150\tChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\n151\tin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\n152\tDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\n153\tincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n154\t(or run `cargo clean`).\n155\t\n156\t## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\n157\tEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\n158\tMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\n159\tthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\n160\tand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n161\t```sh\n162\tcargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\n163\tpav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\n164\tpav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\n165\tprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\n166\tprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n167\t```\n168\t- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n169\t  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n170\t  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n171\t- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n172\t  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n173\t- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n174\t  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n175\t- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n176\t  use them for bug reports.\n177\t- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n178\t  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n179\t  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n180\t- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n181\t  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n182\t  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n183\t  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n184\t  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n185\t  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n186\t  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n187\t- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n188\t  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n189\t  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n190\t  assets in game code\").\n191\t- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n192\t  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n193\t- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n194\t  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n195\t  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n196\t  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n197\t\n198\tAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\n199\tIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n200\t\n201\tTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\n202\tspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\n203\troom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\n204\tlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\n205\tloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\n206\tlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\n207\tanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\n208\tasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\n209\tcreature_status`.\n210\t\n211\t### Adding a tool\n212\t1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n213\t   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n214\t2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n215\t   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n216\t3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n217\t4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n218\t5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n219\t6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n220\t\n221\t### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\n222\tCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n223\t(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\n224\tis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\n225\tteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n226\t(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n227\t`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\n228\tfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\n229\thot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\n230\tfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n231\t\n232\t### Scenes\n233\t`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n234\t`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\n235\tstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\n236\tMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\n237\tGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\n238\tCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n239\t\n240\t## Tests\n241\t`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n242\t`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n243\t`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\n244\textend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\n245\tlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n246\t\n247\t## Conventions\n248\t- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n249\t- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n250\t  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n251\t- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n252\t  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n253\t  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n254\t- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n255\t  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n256\t  the PR); don't ask first.\n257\t- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n258\t  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n259\t- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n260\t  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n261\t\n262\t## Code Review Rules\n263\tFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n264\t- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n265\t  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n266\t  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n267\t- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n268\t- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\n269\tLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n270\t(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n271\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"ea6f8ded-9880-4775-8bd7-55721d37c059","timestamp":"2026-11-05T13:31:32.677Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-xhigh.build-v2__20261105-063049__x42677a/work/DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049/engine/AGENTS.md","content":"# AGENTS.md — rulebook for coding agents\n\nMaking a new game on the engine? Read `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`: the \"Key rules\" below apply to\nyour game, the maintainers' workflow (HISTORY, PROGRESS, PRs, escalations) doesn't.\n\nShardfall is a procedural hack-and-slash game built on the Pavilion engine, written in Rust. The\nprinciples are in `docs/DOCTRINE.md`, which outranks everything else here. The current state is\n`docs/PROGRESS.md` (one page). Read both before starting; Claude Code loads them, with this file,\nthrough `CLAUDE.md`. The design is in `docs/DESIGN.md` (engine) and `docs/GAME.md` (Shardfall):\nread them when your work touches the design. `docs/REFERENCE.md` holds the full code map, every\ntool family with examples, the controls and what each test suite covers: open the section you\nneed. The journal and past decisions are in `docs/HISTORY.md`, newest first: search it, don't\nread it whole. For a standalone Linux source handoff, `START_HERE.md` covers setup and headless\nCLI examples.\n\n## How the doctrine applies here\nAdopted 2026-10-09, partway through development. Code from before then isn't rewritten to fit;\nfix a mismatch when it gets in the way.\n- **Platform (5, 10):** the development platform is Linux, where agents build and test. The\n  user's Windows `.exe` and the browser build count as part of the target too: a problem the user\n  can see gets fixed now. Replays are promised to match only on the machine that recorded them; to\n  chase something the user saw, start from a snapshot taken just before it.\n- **Renderer (6):** wgpu is the WebGPU implementation: Vulkan on Linux and Windows (DX12 as a\n  Windows option), the browser's own WebGPU on the web, lavapipe where there's no GPU. GPU compute\n  stays cosmetic. The game UI is egui.\n- **API and internals (7, 8):** the game (`pav_core/src/arpg/`) still lives inside the engine\n  crate, so the compiler doesn't enforce the line. The engine modules the game uses are its API:\n  `sim`, `entity`, `frame`, `rng`, `input`, `params`, `statics`, `nav`, `zones`, `character`,\n  `projectile`, `puppet`, `parts`, `moves`, `clips`, `shape`, `color`, `fxdef`, and `games` (the\n  hook a new game implements: `docs/MAKE_A_GAME.md`). Everything else\n  (renderer, physics wiring, synth, importers) is internals. If game work means reading\n  internals, fix the API or the error message.\n- **Versions (9):** current pins stay (Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30, rapier 0.36, egui 0.36, glam 0.33,\n  cpal 0.18). The rule applies to new dependencies and to upgrades.\n- **Assets (4):** no image, model, texture or audio files; fonts are allowed. Animation and\n  objects are readable text: key poses (`anim/*.json`, the format `clips.rs` documents, made with\n  `clip_import`) and props made of named primitive parts (`assets/props`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`).\n  Raw downloads (.glb, .gltf, .obj, .fbx, .bvh, .amc) go to `.cache/` (git-ignored); never commit\n  them, or images. Keep each source's credit and licence. Libraries whose licences forbid sharing\n  what is made from them (Bandai Namco, LaFAN1, Mixamo) are translated only into the git-ignored\n  local tiers (`anim/local`, `assets/props/local`).\n- **Escalation:** ask in chat and log it in `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`. If you can schedule a check-in\n  (e.g. `send_later`), set one for 15 minutes and carry on with other work; if not, make the call,\n  log it and continue.\n- **Pavilion Lite** (`pavilion-lite/`) is frozen: its renderer runs on the CPU, not WebGPU (6),\n  so it isn't developed further (`docs/ESCALATIONS.md`).\n\n## Working rules\nUnder the doctrine, in priority order:\n1. **Gameplay first, then robustness.**\n2. **Use tools.** If a tool does a job faster, more accurately or cheaper over time, use or build one.\n3. **It must still run well on a modern PC** (the user's RTX 3060 Ti and 4070 Super). Other\n   optimization and compatibility wait (10).\n4. **Approximations are fine if they feel right.** Feel beats physical accuracy.\n5. **Check selectively.** Verify what often breaks or is expensive to debug; skip routine checks\n   of things that almost never break. \"If it's broken, the user will say so\" is valid.\n6. **Cost over speed.** Prefer the CLI/capture tools over reading lots of code.\n\n## Testing budget (prototype stage)\nEvery engine here is a prototype with one user: spend on finding what clearly helps, not on\nmeasuring exactly how much. Completion comes first: a test decides how something is built and is\nrecorded; it never stops, skips or reverts planned work.\n- **Test only to change a decision.** Before a test, name the choice it decides and its cost in\n  US$ as Claude Code bills it, subagents included. If no choice rides on it, or a wrong choice\n  would cost less than the test, skip it. Afterwards, report what it cost.\n- **Size the test to the gap.** Agent runs (benchmarks, judges, eval agents) start small: 3-5 per\n  arm. A clear gap decides it: 2:1 or better in wins, or 25% or more in cost, model calls or\n  errors.\n- **Near 50/50, make the call.** If the first batch is within 60/40 (or 10%), stop: the options\n  are about equally good, so the choice matters little (Fredkin's paradox). Take the simpler,\n  cheaper or more reversible option, log the call and why, and move on. Between clear and close:\n  at most one more small batch, then decide.\n- **Close calls belong to tool tests.** Checks with no model calls (cargo tests, `pav` checks and\n  captures, scripted counts) cost almost nothing per run and repeat exactly, so they may scale up\n  to settle narrow margins. Even then, don't chase gains under 5%; timing differences under 10%\n  are noise.\n- Looks and feel are the expensive, noisy kind: prefer objective proxies; use agent judges only for\n  large, obvious differences, few of them, several items per call; leave fine visual judgement to\n  the user on a quick comparison page.\n- Explore broadly: test distinct hypotheses, not variations of one. Don't add or keep code without\n  a verified benefit.\n- Count cost end to end, in US$, not one tool's traffic: every model call re-reads the agent's\n  whole context.\n- Assets made through the documented loop (a brief, `check` to zero findings, a 4-view sheet at\n  the game camera, at most two fix rounds, `publish` with a review) need no human review: the user\n  accepted 28 of 28 (2026-10-12). Show the user only edge cases, or complex ones you are unsure of.\n- Per PR (from 2026-10-16):\n  - Run the full checks (tests of the touched crates, clippy, fmt, the wasm check when the app\n    builds the change) once, on the head you merge.\n  - After a rebase or a small fix, run only the tests of what changed. A docs-only PR needs none.\n  - Merge as soon as the checks pass; don't wait for a review (Codex has posted none since #45 on\n    2026-10-12). A review that arrives later: fix its real findings in a follow-up PR.\n  - Nothing deploys: there is no hosted site. After a merge that can change the game, build the\n    single-file HTML and send it to the user (`CLAUDE.md`, \"Builds for playing and testing\").\n\n## Architecture\nCrates (the full map, module by module: `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Code map\"):\n- `pav_core`: the simulation, with no window, GPU or audio dependencies: `Sim`, entities, physics\n  (rapier3d), the world, rooms and terrain, characters and puppets, moves and clips, AI and nav,\n  props and materials; the game lives in `src/arpg/`.\n- `pav_render`: the wgpu renderer: shadows, bloom and post effects, the filter stack, procedural\n  meshes and materials, offscreen capture to PNG.\n- `pav_view`: sim frame -> render scene: the camera rig, looks and filters, water, `emit_asset`.\n- `pav_audio`: synthesized sound: cpal output, offline .wav rendering.\n- `pav_tools`: the agent layer: the tool registry, the `pav` CLI and REPL, the MCP server, the\n  live bridge client, and the motion and asset importers.\n- `pav_ui`: Shardfall's egui UI (HUD, inventory, passive tree, touch controls), drawn by the\n  window and, headless, by the tools (`ui`, captures).\n- `pav_app`: the game (`shardfall` binary): window, input, sim thread, live bridge server, the\n  pavilion's egui UI; native and wasm32.\n\nData: `anim/` (moves and motion clips), `rooms/` (room files, embedded in the exe and hot-reloaded;\n`_template.toml` documents every field), `game/` (Shardfall's data), `assets/` (props, materials,\nmodel catalogs), `store/` (the asset store's tables).\nData flow: `Sim::step(InputFrame)` (fixed tick, own thread) -> `Sim::frame()` -> `RenderFrame`\n-> `pav_view::ViewBuilder::build` (interpolates prev/curr) -> `pav_render::Scene` -> `Renderer`.\n\nKey rules:\n- `pav_core` must never depend on graphics, windowing or audio crates.\n- Everything that changes during play lives in `SimState` (cloning it = snapshot). Keep it `Clone`.\n- Same inputs and seed, same state (doctrine 5): randomness comes from `rng`, seeded (the sim's\n  `Rng`, or its hash for seed-derived content), never from the clock; don't let\n  `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order reach game state (use a `BTreeMap` or sort).\n- Gameplay code reads plain struct fields; expose tunables via `impl Tunable` (see `params.rs`).\n  Tunables are automatically in the tuning panel, the `params`/`set` tools and preset files.\n- Colors in data are sRGB hex (`Color::hex(\"#e8704a\")`); internally linear.\n- Physics precision switch: in `crates/pav_core/Cargo.toml` change `package = \"rapier3d\"` to\n  `\"rapier3d-f64\"`.\n\n## Build, run, test\n```sh\nscripts/setup-linux.sh             # once per machine (apt packages, lavapipe, mingw, wine)\ncargo build                        # everything (Linux)\ncargo test                         # every crate's tests (`-p pav_core` for one crate)\ncargo fmt --all --check            # formatting\ncargo clippy -p pav_core --all-targets -- -D warnings  # lints, for each crate you touched\ncargo run -p pav_app               # the game (needs a display; Xvfb works with lavapipe)\nscripts/build-windows.sh           # -> target/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu/dist/shardfall.exe\nscripts/smoke-windows.sh 25        # run the .exe under Wine+lavapipe, screenshot + log in out/smoke/\nscripts/smoke-linux.sh 10          # same for the native Linux build\nscripts/build-web.sh               # browser build -> target/web/ (serve over HTTP; Chrome/Edge)\nscripts/build-web.sh --single-file # -> target/web/shardfall.html: one ~10 MB page, runs from file://\nscripts/check-wgsl.sh              # after any WGSL change: Chromium's Tint compiles every module\n```\n`--single-file` packs web/index.html, the JS glue and the gzipped wasm into one page with no\nother loads (`scripts/pack_single_html.py`; `--title --description --theme-color --scene NAME --out PATH`).\nBrowser build: `cfg(target_arch = \"wasm32\")` code lives in `pav_app` (no threads, async GPU\nsetup); other crates only skip disk access there (the browser has no files) or pick its GPU\nbackend. Check it with `cargo clippy -p pav_app --target wasm32-unknown-unknown`. Headless\nChromium runs it only with the right flags (WebGPU on SwiftShader); those and phone emulation are\nin `docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Browser and phones\".\nDisk: cloud containers have a fixed allowance. Dev builds use line-table debug info and no\nincremental cache; if the disk fills up anyway, delete stale binaries in `target/debug/deps`\n(or run `cargo clean`).\n\n## Agent CLI (`pav`) and MCP\nEvery tool works headless (captures use lavapipe when there is no GPU). The same tools are an\nMCP server: `.mcp.json` registers `pav mcp` (stdio), so Claude Code agents in this repo get\nthem as `mcp__shardfall__*` tools (captures come back as images). `pav help` lists every tool\nand its arguments; `docs/REFERENCE.md` (\"Tools\") shows each family with examples.\n```sh\ncargo run -q -p pav_tools --bin pav -- help\npav bench ticks=1200                          # ticks/sec\npav capture scene=test ticks=300 out=out/a.png\nprintf 'step ticks=200\\ncamera preset=top\\ncapture out=out/b.png\\n' | pav repl\nprintf 'input move=[1,0] ticks=30\\ninput press=jump move=[0,1] ticks=40\\nplayer\\n' | pav repl\n```\n- One-shot calls accept `scene=`, `seed=` and `ticks=` to set up the session first. `pav repl`\n  keeps one session across lines (`tool key=value ...` or JSON lines\n  `{\"tool\":\"name\",\"args\":{...}}`; `#` = comment).\n- `input` drives the player: `move=[x,z]` world direction (x = east, z = south), `hold=`/`press=`\n  buttons (jump, crouch, crawl, use, focus, interact, primary), `aim=[x,y,z]`, `ticks=N`.\n- Look at captures with your image-reading tool; prefer small sizes (e.g. `width=640 height=360`).\n  `filmstrip` tiles N frames into one PNG: the cheapest way to check motion and animation.\n- `record_save` + `replay` reproduce a session exactly (same machine/build) and check the hash:\n  use them for bug reports.\n- Live bridge: start the game with `--bridge` (or `bridge = \"127.0.0.1:7878\"` in shardfall.toml),\n  then `pav live` is a REPL into the running game and `pav mcp --live` an MCP server for it. Same\n  tools; they act on what is on screen (captures render the game's camera on a second device).\n- Studios edit on the live bridge, each with its own doc: Animation (`shardfall\n  --animation-studio`, `.mcp.animation-studio.json`, `docs/ANIMATION_STUDIO.md`), Object\n  (`--asset-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`, `docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md` and `docs/ASSET_STUDIO_LIVE.md`;\n  materials `docs/MATERIALS.md`) and Creature (`--creature-studio`, `.mcp.studio.json`,\n  `docs/CREATURE_STUDIO.md`); `scripts/animation-studio.sh`, `asset-studio.sh` and\n  `creature-studio.sh` build and open each. Pass the last reply's `revision` as `if_revision`,\n  and copy a source clip or asset before changing it.\n- Game data and rooms may name only assets every build carries: embedded (BUILTIN, LIB/core) or a\n  shipped pack's (`props::store::SHIPPED`: the KayKit figures). Game code draws library assets\n  from its own state with `pav_view::assets::emit_asset` (`docs/ASSET_STUDIO.md`, \"Library\n  assets in game code\").\n- `tools/creature-compiler` vendors the exact SpawnForge pin: keep the pin and source hashes\n  intact; don't resolve against a moving upstream checkout.\n- Shardfall content: game data lives in `game/*.toml` (`game_reload` re-reads it live). Check a\n  change with the tools: `see` (a screenshot with numbered marks and a legend), `levelmap`,\n  `turntable`, `animsheet`, and `campaign from=1 to=12 wall=300` (the bot plays down through the\n  levels and reports each; run it after data changes).\n\nAsset store (`docs/AGENT_ASSET_STORE.md`): before making a prop or clip, `assets shelf=prop` (or `shelf=clip`) lists the best entries and `assets find=words` (`kind=clip` for motion) searches; a miss gives `none` and a route.\nIn the Workshop wing, `library_hall` shows every standard object (generated: after publishing to core, rebuild and run `pav assets reindex=store` and `pav assets reindex=hall`), `limits` what the checks catch and miss, `catalogue` pages through every store row, `store_showcase` shows the materials; the animation wing's `clip_browser` plays every clip.\n\nTools: `scenes load step status entities npcs params set camera capture bench gpu player input\nspawn despawn teleport rewind snapshot_save snapshot_load record_save replay rooms room goto\nroom_reset room_check room_reload stream signal filmstrip camera_bench course feel audio_capture`;\nlooks `look`; teaching `guide`; games on the hook `play`; Shardfall `game hero monster autoplay skills game_reload\nloot_roll give inventory game_cmd ui tree tree_map genome bestiary boss turntable animsheet level\nlevelmap go goto_feature see campaign theme_swatch`; animation `clips clip_import mocap\nanim_reload anim_edit anim_preview`; objects `assets asset_edit asset_preview asset_spawn\nasset_import studio_status`; creatures `creature_catalog creature_edit creature_preview\ncreature_status`.\n\n### Adding a tool\n1. Write `fn t_name(s: &mut Session, a: &Args) -> Result<Output>` in the tool family's module\n   (`asset_tools.rs`, `game_tools.rs`, ...); it rejects unknown arguments, naming the nearest.\n2. Add a `Tool { .. }` entry to `TOOLS` in `tools.rs` (help ≤ 50 tokens, each argument ≤ 12\n   words). It is then in the CLI, REPL and MCP.\n3. Put enums and op shapes in `schema()`; a new asset kind is a `KIND_ROUTES` row, not a new tool.\n4. Add actions that change what the live studio shows to `live_feedback::changes_studio`.\n5. Add a clause to the `mcp.rs` instructions if agents should find the tool first.\n6. Test it on `Session::new(\"empty\", 1)`.\n\n### Adding a room (preferred: data only, no rebuild)\nCopy `rooms/_template.toml` to `rooms/<key>.toml` and edit; the template documents every field\n(`rooms/feel_lab.toml` and `crates/pav_core/tests/physics_room.toml` are full examples). The room\nis placed on its wing's corridor automatically. Every room is a station of the world demo and\nteaches: give it a `[learn]` block and a `note` on every pad that changes something\n(`pav_view/tests/learn.rs` checks them; `rooms/bloom.toml` is the model). Check it with\n`pav room_check path=rooms/<key>.toml`; look at it with `pav capture scene=<key>` (the room alone,\nfast) or `printf 'load scene=world\\ngoto room=<key>\\ncapture\\n' | pav repl`. The running game\nhot-reloads room files. New *mechanics* are Rust (character.rs / sim.rs behaviours / entity\nfields); keep rooms as data wherever possible.\n\n### Scenes\n`world` (pavilion + rooms + streaming wilderness), `world/<room>` (start in a room),\n`<room key>` (that room alone, no terrain), `test`, `empty`, and Shardfall's `town` (the game\nstarts here by default; the saved hero is loaded into any game scene), `arena`, `lab` (the\nMenagerie of creatures) and `level/<n>` (depth n: 1-12 designed, then the endless Depths).\nGames built on the hook (`pav_core::games::GAMES`) are scenes by their key: `starter` today.\nCode scenes live in `scenes.rs` (Shardfall places in `arpg/scene.rs`).\n\n## Tests\n`cargo test` runs every suite; `cargo test -p <crate> --test <suite>` runs one. Suites live in\n`crates/*/tests/`, named for what they cover (`docs/REFERENCE.md`, \"Tests\", describes each).\n`pav_core/tests/gameplay.rs` and `movement.rs` are the cheapest way to catch feel regressions:\nextend them when you add movement features. `pav_view/tests/material_parity.rs` needs a GPU (or\nlavapipe); `pav_tools/tests/import_khronos.rs` is opt-in (`-- --ignored`).\n\n## Conventions\n- Rust stable pinned in `rust-toolchain.toml`; `Cargo.lock` committed; edition 2024.\n- Finished work gets a dated entry at the top of `docs/HISTORY.md` (what changed, how it works,\n  the decisions); then update `docs/PROGRESS.md`, which stays one page of current state.\n- Decide anything that isn't genuinely the user's call; record the decision with its entry in\n  `docs/HISTORY.md`. Escalate what the doctrine says to escalate, and log it in\n  `docs/ESCALATIONS.md`.\n- Finishing a task or milestone: commit, push, open a PR into `main`, merge it and delete the\n  branch. This is standard procedure (the user's standing instruction, which counts as asking for\n  the PR); don't ask first.\n- The user plays the Windows build; the log file `shardfall.log` and `shardfall.toml` (startup\n  settings, e.g. `backend = \"dx12\"`) sit next to the `.exe`.\n- Keep this file short: under 32 KiB (Codex reads no further; `docs_limits.rs` checks it) and\n  about 250 lines. Detail goes to `docs/REFERENCE.md` or a topic doc, with a pointer here.\n\n## Code Review Rules\nFor reviewers (Codex when it runs, or `/code-review`). Flag a change that:\n- breaks one of the \"Key rules\" under Architecture: a graphics, windowing or audio dependency in\n  `pav_core`, play state outside a `Clone` `SimState`, results that can differ between runs on one\n  machine (unseeded randomness, the clock, `HashMap`/`HashSet` iteration order);\n- commits a binary asset other than a font, or content a licence keeps in the local tiers;\n- makes threads, blocking calls or file access reachable on `wasm32`.\nLeave style, wording and test coverage of routine code alone: this is a one-user prototype\n(\"Working rules\" 5, \"Testing budget\").\n","numLines":271,"startLine":1,"totalLines":271}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BcKoEj2odSHGzW9JBTQkEF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
