{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgkU7wdSpiqcQye49ZF8T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Diorama Center: Second Opinion is finished\n\nAll 73 automated tests pass in one run on the final build, and the handed-in file is byte-identical to the page they ran against. My first \"done\" was premature: checking the plan line by line turned up real problems, now fixed. No person has played it yet, and nothing is committed to git.\n\n- **Game:** `DioramaCenterSecondOpinion-TraumaCenterSecondOpinion-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261105-063049.html` (12 MB, one file)\n- **Exit interview:** `exit-interview.json`\n- **Dev files:** the folder of the same name, source only (`engine/target` is deleted). The designer guide is `engine/crates/pav_core/src/games/surgery/content/README.md`.\n\n**Controls**\n- **Aim:** right stick or mouse\n- **Operate:** R2, Cross or left click\n- **Pick a tool:** left-stick flick, L1/R1, 1–8, the wheel, or click the tray\n- **Gift:** Triangle or Space (hold, release on the beat)\n- **Let go:** Circle, right click or Q\n- **Chart:** Square or Tab\n- **Paddles:** L2+R2, both mouse buttons or F\n- **Pause and Dev Mode:** Options or Esc; Dev Mode is in the pause menu\n\n## What the second pass fixed\n- **Pad play:** the stick-and-assist test skipped cases 4 and 7. It now covers all eight, and they pass.\n- **Ranks rewarded slow play:** pumping the IV paid more than surgery, so skill 0.3 could reach ENCORE while skill 1.0 got S. IV doses, repeat heat passes and unplanned wounds now pay a quarter and build no chain.\n- **Difficulty spike:** Case 4's bursts drained faster than the IV could heal, which made it harder than the final case. I eased them and raised drain on cases 3, 5, 6 and 7.\n- **Skipped twist:** freezing every ember first bypassed Case 6's merge. It now always happens.\n- **Test bot:** slow settings swapped tools without finishing anything, which made Resident look impossible. It now plays more like a person, so skill numbers can't be compared with earlier runs.\n- **Test coverage:** per-case and campaign wins now run on the 10 seeds the plan asks for, and a new test requires skill 0.4 on Resident to win every case.\n\n**Balance now (bot only):**\n- **Attending, 20 seeds:** 1.0 and 0.7 win every case, 0.4 loses cases 4 and 6 outright and wins Case 7 only 3 times in 20, and 0 always loses.\n- **Ranks:** on average they rise with skill in all eight cases; Code Blue isn't covered by that check.\n\n## What wasn't or couldn't be verified\n- **No human has played it.** Feel, readability and difficulty come from bots, scripts and screenshots. The \"person\" evidence is a bot I tuned to act like one.\n- **Length is unmeasured and may fall short of \"about an hour\".** The bot takes about 16, 23 and 30 minutes for the eight operations at skills 0.5, 0.4 and 0.3 on Resident, against the plan's 43. I'd guess 35–50 minutes for a first-time player including story, and I chose not to pad cases to reach the target.\n- **Audio was never heard.** It was only checked by rendering it and reading levels.\n- **Graphics ran on software rendering only,** at about 1–10 fps. A real GPU, controller and iPhone are untested.\n- **Wide shot:** the puppets are the weakest art in the game, and the front one shows ghosting I can't judge at this frame rate.\n- **Hub coverage is partial.** All four harness scenarios pass, but the shim's ready fallback and keepalive help slow machines get through them. Real gameplay sync was only tested with two players.\n- **Test retries:** the browser and hub runners retry after a memory crash and don't log it, so some passes may have taken more than one attempt.\n- **After the 73/73 run:** I fixed the screenshot test, reran it alone (it passed), and edited the designer guide. Neither is part of the game page.\n\n## Changes from the plan\n- Case 6's ring has the Laser where the Scanner would be, so the furnace can use every tool.\n- The closing dressing steps aside when you pick another tool, because locking it caused a stuck state at low vitals.\n- Each case has its own `drain` setting.\n- Lacrimosa sacs swell over 26 nominal beats in Case 4 and 14 in Case 7, not the plan's 8–12, which a person couldn't keep up with.\n- IV doses are rated as planned but pay a quarter of the points.\n\n---\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-05 06:30:49\n- **Source:** Trauma Center: Second Opinion\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Surgical action (a pointer-precision arcade game under a ticking clock), framed by a visual-novel medical melodrama, with drop-in co-op\n- **Perspective:** Operations: a fixed surgeon's-eye view of the operative field, 73 degrees from the horizon, with short camera pushes. Story: a staged paper theatre, straight on, plus 3D wide shots of the operating room\n- **Tech:** Pavilion engine (shardfall repository, commit 49ef2ba, changed for this game): Rust 1.98.1, wgpu 30 on WebGPU, egui 0.36, rapier 0.36, glam 0.33, cpal 0.18, gilrs 0.11, winit 0.30, WebAssembly, one HTML file. AI Hub session client 1.4.0\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's cel bands, ink outlines and bloom. The field is one mesh painted by per-vertex colour with stylised tissue; instruments and parasites are SDF solids; story characters are layered, extruded spline cards like a motion comic. Barlow Condensed for all text. No image or model files\n- **Animation:** The engine's sim-driven procedural motion and its puppet clips for the wide shots, plus new procedural animation: instruments squeezing and plunging, tissue beating on the heartbeat, parasites stepping on the beat, paper rigs that blink, breathe, sway and flap their mouths\n- **Audio:** All written in code in a bus mixer: about 40 effect cues, a note sequencer with composed tracks whose tempo follows the heart, a crisis layer and a motif per strain in its own meter (3/4, 6/8, 5/4, 7/8), and Animalese-style formant voices per character. Music defaults to 10%\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1: one Code Blue round in shared-world mode, 1 to 4 seats, host authoritative, the game's own card. Outside a session the game is unaffected. Local co-op for up to 4 pads\n- **Testing:** Headless Rust tests (rules, bot on every case and the whole campaign, coverage, balance targets, determinism, shell navigation, audio rendered offline) and scripted browser tests in Chromium with software WebGPU (stub DualSense, phone profile, analyser tap, hub harness, two-copy session), behind one command. Screenshots only for what scripts cannot check. No subagents or workflows\n- **Research:** Done by the plan: Trauma Center: Second Opinion (Atlus, Wii launch title, 2006), an enhanced remake of the DS Under the Knife; pointer aiming and Nunchuk flick tool-select; two doctors with different gifts; rank scoring; later New Blood added co-op. No further research by me\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n## Testing Feedback\n- **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35. The ones that mattered: the tumor's ring cut was never spawned, so Case 3 could not be finished; the closing dressing locked out the IV, so a low-vitals player was stuck until death; touch taps missed the Settings button because the canvas was not sized the way the pointer events assumed; slow frames kept hub copies from calling ready in time; the title clipped on a phone; the attract timer ran slow on slow frames; menu repeats overshot on stalled frames; the campaign never used the IV; glasses hid the eyes; the wide-shot camera sat behind a wall. Found late, from a table of every difficulty and skill: pumping the IV paid more than surgery, so slow play ranked above good play; slow bots swapped tools without finishing anything; bursts in Case 4 drained faster than the IV could heal, which made it harder than the final case; and freezing every ember first skipped the merge twist in Case 6. Plus a dozen balance and test-harness fixes.\n- **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 4: the unfinishable Case 3 tumor, the dressing lock, phone taps missing the only button on that screen, and four-player hub rounds failing the harness. The IV score farm broke the ranks, not the game, so I don't count it here.\n- **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The bot playing every case and the whole campaign with coverage counters, together with the balance tool. It runs in seconds, found the unfinishable case and the dressing lock, and told me what to change after every rule edit. Printing wins and times per difficulty and skill, rather than only pass or fail against targets, is what exposed the score farm and the difficulty spike.\n- **What was the least helpful test you ran?** Browser tests that waited on real time before I made them count frames: software WebGPU on this busy machine ran at about 1 to 10 frames a second, so they mostly measured the machine. The WebKit iPhone load also told me little, because it stops at the page's WebGPU message.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n- **What did you like most about the engine?** Everything runs and draws headless. A deterministic sim behind one small hook, `pav capture` for a real frame in about a second, and `DynamicMesh` plus SDF solids meant I could paint a whole beating organ and see it without a display.\n- **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, in small ways. Leftover events from the last scene turned into wild distortions in my first captures, which looked like a rendering bug. I guessed the story camera's distance and put it behind a wall. A new dependency needs one build without --locked. And big release compiles were killed for memory on this shared machine until I built with fewer jobs from a script that waits for free memory.\n- **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The game's own frame path and shell in product.rs and pav_ui: focus-list menus, prompts that follow the device, per-pad input with analog triggers, the HUD, and the hub bridge. The plate renderer and the bus mixer with its sequencer and formant voices came next.\n- **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Getting out of the demo's way. App::frame carries about 800 lines of demo behaviour, and there is no per-game camera, sound or UI seam, so a game that wants its own screens has to branch above all of it and reopen several crates.\n- **What did you change in the engine, and why?** input.rs: surgeon slots and a game command in InputFrame, for replays and co-op. frame.rs and sim.rs: a Cue event and a per-game frame, because the hook cannot draw. build.rs: a per-game emit at the end of the build. session.rs and headless.rs: the game's camera and shell, so the tools see what the window sees. pav_audio: three buses, formant filters, vibrato, a raised voice limit, cues, music and voices. app.rs, simhost.rs and settings.rs: a branch to the game's own frame path and a command queue. pack_single_html.py and index.html: the hub client, tags, shim and About block.\n- **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Give games a real seam for their own frame: a trait with camera, draw, sound and UI hooks that the window and the headless tools both call, with the demo's behaviour living behind it instead of in App::frame.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n- **What did you like most about this prompt?** The plan's check marks. Knowing which values, look and protocol shape had already been tried on this machine saved a great deal of searching, and the plan's clear Never cut list made every trade-off easy.\n- **What was confusing?** Mostly small things: the task lets me pick genre and perspective while the plan fixes them, and the plan says it never names the deliverable while its build line needs one. The first was settled by the plan's own note that the task's requirements win.\n- **What was the hardest thing to do?** Passing the hub harness with four software-rendered copies on a shared machine with little free memory, because the protocol's deadlines do not wait for slow frames. The sheer breadth was the other: a whole game, engine seams, audio, co-op and tools in one run.\n- **What would you change in this prompt?** State a memory budget for builds and browsers alongside the port rules, since other agents compete for it. And say whether a hub round must pass on software rendering, because that drove real design decisions such as the keepalive and the draw throttle."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":836,"cache_read_input_tokens":287398,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":836},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"c977b315-ae43-486a-927e-a4e338a129a4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:11:54.187Z","request_id":"req_011CgkU7wEstXDTJLdaKpUrP"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says. Done when: Everything in PLAN.md that can be done on this computer is done, and everything TASK.md asks you to hand in is in this folder.]: Not satisfied on the transcript. PLAN.md's own completion bar is 'every test passes; the campaign and every case can be finished by a person with a pad, or with a mouse', and the transcript shows that was never established. Nothing has been played by a person, and the final message says so. The audio was 'never heard', graphics ran only on software rendering, and the plan's 'about an hour' length target was not reached (the bot takes about 16 to 30 minutes against a plan target of 43). The hub coverage is described as partial, with the harness passing partly because of the shim's fallback and keepalive. Cases 4 and 7 were only pad-tested after the final turn. The handed-in items are present: the .html and exit-interview.json are in the work root and the exit interview parses. The remaining gaps are real plan items that the transcript shows as not met, so the stop condition is not met."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"9bd98efc-0a45-40dc-861a-22d193bb6552","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:12:02.575Z","isSynthetic":true}
