{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015Vsbo62EHvQq1wsekDkCiL","type":"tool_result","content":"## case0_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.8} ]\nnarrator/-: Port Verity. Mercy Harbor General. The storm has been in the city for nine days, and the city has stopped expecting anything else.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: Second year. Four hundred and twelve nights in this building, and I can still tell you which radiator is flat by a semitone. It is the one outside Theatre Three. It has never once been fixed.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Doctor.\nmara/alarmed: Bashir. Where's Dr. Oyelaran? The board says there's a hand on the way up. A forearm. The board says \"attending.\"\nbashir/stern: The attending is elbow-deep in a bus driver. This one's yours, Doctor.\nmara/alarmed: Mine. As in... by myself. Solo. With the hands I have.\nbashir/neutral: Those are the ones I'd bring.\nmara/alarmed: I once dropped a retractor into a patient's lunch.\nbashir/soft: He enjoyed the lunch. A net-mender off Pier Nine, Doctor. A winch let go in the wind. He has hooks in him to the elbow and he is apologising to the porters.\nmara/soft: My father tuned pianos, Bashir. In this city, in this weather. He used to say a string doesn't care how frightened you are. It only cares where you put your hand.\nbashir/neutral: Then put it where I tell you. Scrub in. I'll hand you everything but the nerve.\n\n## case0_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/soft: Your hands were steady.\nmara/soft: They weren't. I was counting his heartbeats.\nbashir/neutral: Most people count sutures.\nmara/neutral: Seventy-two. He was seventy-two when we started. A little sharp around the second hook, then he came back. Did you hear it?\nbashir/neutral: I heard a monitor, Doctor.\nmara/soft: Yes. Of course. So did I.\nnarrator/-: Anton Deverell's hand opened and closed at 4:10 in the morning. He asked who to thank. Nobody on the night shift was willing to be named.\n\n## case1_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Harbour pilot, Doctor. Rosalind Pike. An appendix. There are first-years who could do this in the dark, and in this building they have.\nmara/neutral: Then why is the gallery lit?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\n  [ {'camera': 'or'} ]\nvoss/stern: Because I am in it, Dr. Quill.\n  [ {'camera': 'paper'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Chief Voss. I didn't... nobody said. It's a routine appendix, Chief.\nvoss/stern: In my hospital, adequate is a kind of malpractice. You closed a forearm last week, and the porters have been telling it like scripture. I came to see whether it was surgery or weather.\nmara/alarmed: Chief, I...\nvoss/neutral: Do not narrate, Doctor. Operate.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nteddy/neutral: Hey! I'm Teddy. I'm the one keeping her asleep, which I'm told is important. I do jokes when I'm scared, so if you hear jokes, that's information.\nbashir/neutral: He has never once stopped joking.\nteddy/soft: See? He gets it.\n\n## case1_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nmara/alarmed: An artery let go. On a routine appendix. On the routine appendix, with her watching. I had my hand in there, Bashir, and I felt it go before it went.\nbashir/neutral: You did.\nmara/neutral: What did she say? Did she say anything?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nvoss/stern: Adequate.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Was that... is that good?\nbashir/soft: Doctor, I have worked here twenty-two years. I have heard her say that twice. The other one has a wing named after him now.\n\n## case2_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 1} ]\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\nnarrator/-: At 2:40 in the morning, Grain Terminal Two stops being a building. They hear it at Mercy Harbor before they see it. The windows on the east side bow in, consider it, and hold.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'right'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: Okay! Okay okay okay. That's eleven. Eleven on trolleys, and they're lining them up by the lifts, and someone's asked me for a form, and I don't think we have that form!\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Who's worst?\nteddy/alarmed: Foreman. Gideon Marr. He went back in. For the lunch tins, Mara. For the night shift's lunch tins. He's got most of a conveyor in his chest.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nbashir/stern: There is no attending, Doctor. There is no theatre, either: we are setting up behind that curtain. This is a corridor with ambitions.\nmara/neutral: Then get me a scanner and get me a light. Teddy. Breathe.\nteddy/soft: I'm breathing. I'm the only one here who's supposed to be doing it for someone else, but I'm breathing.\n\n## case2_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: He'll live. All eleven of them will live. Someone should tell the terminal it didn't win.\nbashir/neutral: Yes, Doctor.\nmara/neutral: You keep looking at me.\nbashir/stern: At 3:06 you said \"now.\" Then the heart stopped. In that order.\nmara/alarmed: I...\nbashir/neutral: I have stood beside a great many surgeons. I don't have a question about it. I want you to know I am not asking.\nmara/soft: Thank you.\nbashir/soft: Eat something. I've left a sandwich on the autoclave. It may be sterile.\n\n## case3_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'atrium'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'vane', 'at': 'right'} ]\nvane/soft: To the night staff of Mercy Harbor. No, don't get up, don't you dare, you've been on your feet since the terminal. I only wanted to see the people who do it.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Mrs. Vane. It's... I'm only here for the canapés. I should be on the ward, I'm not really dressed for a wing.\nvane/soft: You must be Dr. Quill. Eleven men from Grain Terminal Two wrote to my Foundation about you. Handwritten. Do you know how rarely anyone handwrites a thing to money?\nmara/soft: I had a good nurse.\nvane/soft: You had good hands. I can always tell. My whole life I've been around people who make things keep time: clockmakers, conductors. You have a lovely ear, Doctor. I do hope you're looked after.\nnarrator/-: Cordelia Vane smiled, and a wing of the hospital smiled with her. It was the warmest thing that had happened to Mara Quill all year.\n  [ {'exit': 'vane'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Doctor. Courier from the Foundation, as it happens. Ines Calloway. A benign mass of the liver: elective, forty minutes, home for breakfast.\nmara/neutral: Benign.\nbashir/neutral: It says so on the film. In three places.\n\n## case3_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nmara/alarmed: It moved. You saw it. It split, and it walked, and it kept time, Bashir, it was keeping time with her heart!\nteddy/alarmed: I would like it noted that I said waltz. On the record. Is there a record?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nvoss/stern: There is no record.\nvoss/stern: Theatre Three is sealed until morning. The film is lost. The specimen was benign. You will wash your hands, you will sign nothing, and you will tell no one what you did not see in this room.\nmara/alarmed: Chief. Time stopped. There was a... it went quiet, everything went quiet, and I could hear...\nvoss/neutral: Yes. I know what you could hear.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\nteddy/soft: Did she just... did she say she knows?\nbashir/neutral: Wash your hands, Doctor.\n\n## case4_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'nightward'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nnarrator/-: Below the morgue level there is a lift the building's plans do not include. It smells of cold iron and old rain. It stops at a floor with no number.\nmara/alarmed: This isn't on the fire map.\nvoss/neutral: Very little of importance is. Mercy Harbor's founders built this theatre for cases that could not exist. We call it the Night Ward, because we are not a poetic profession.\nvoss/stern: We call it Requiem. Recombinant Enzymatic Quiescent Immuno-Evasive Microfilaria. It is the only organism ever designed to keep time.\nmara/alarmed: Keep time for what?\nvoss/stern: For the end of you, Doctor.\nvoss/neutral: It entrains to the host's heartbeat and it waits. Each strain plays in its own meter. Introit, three-four, you have met. There are others, named by someone with a sense of occasion.\nmara/alarmed: Someone made this?\nvoss/neutral: Tonight's patient you know. Rosalind Pike came back for a follow-up, and someone seeded her in recovery, in my hospital, on my floor. She is on the table. No guidance this time. There is nobody left who knows how, except, it would appear, you.\nmara/soft: I can't hear it from here. I could if I were closer.\nvoss/stern: Then go and be closer.\n\n## case4_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\nmara/soft: She's breathing. Sixty-eight. Lovely and flat. Chief, I can't seem to...\nmara/alarmed: I can't open my hands. They've locked. I can't feel... it's so quiet in here. Why is it so quiet?\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\n  [ {'exit': 'mara'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.5} ]\nnarrator/-: Dr. Mara Quill fell the way a piano falls: all at once, and with every string sounding.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/alarmed: Exhaustion. Her pressure's in the floor. She held the Hush for eleven minutes on no sleep. Chief, nobody's built for this.\nvoss/stern: No.\nvoss/neutral: There are three more strains, Mr. Haddad, and I have one surgeon, and she is on the floor. Get your coat.\nbashir/neutral: Where are we going, Chief?\nvoss/stern: To the bus depot on Cooper Street. We need a second opinion.\n\n## case5_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'depot'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\nnarrator/-: Three in the morning, the Cooper Street bus depot. The last route stopped running in the spring. The tea machine has not been told.\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\npet/stern: This tea is an insult. I've been coming here for it for six years. Don't sit there, young man, that's the bench I'm disappointed on.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Dr. Nwosu. My name is Bashir Haddad. I was told you would say no.\npet/neutral: I say no to everyone. Look. No.\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\npet/stern: Ah. Ottoline. I smelled the starch from the road.\nvoss/stern: Perpetua.\npet/stern: You've got old. It suits you. It's the first thing that has.\nvoss/neutral: I have a longshoreman with a shattered femur, and on his second film something is growing in the marrow, and it is keeping time.\npet/alarmed: ...In what meter?\nvoss/stern: That's why I'm here.\npet/neutral: I have buried better surgeons than you, and I liked them less. Look at this hand. Go on, look at it. It shakes. It has shaken since the Ostend ferry. I haven't held a scalpel in nine years.\nbashir/soft: I'll hold it for you, Doctor. Until you want it.\npet/soft: ...Hm. You're the one who says Doctor.\npet/stern: Scalpel. Oh, don't look so pleased. And somebody bring that machine's tea, I'm not done hating it.\n\n## case5_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'left'} ]\npet/soft: Nine years. And the hand, Ottoline. You saw the hand. For ten seconds I had the hands I had at thirty-three, and there were two of them on the outside of me, and they knew what they were doing.\nvoss/neutral: You always did have a flair for appearing twice.\npet/neutral: You knew it would come back. That's why you came for me and not for someone with a licence. How long have you known about this strain, Ottoline?\nvoss/stern: Longer than you have been disappointed in that tea.\npet/stern: Don't you be arch with me. You've gone a terrible colour. Ottoline. When did you last... Ottoline?\nvoss/alarmed: It's only that the fifth beat... the fifth beat is always so very\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.6} ]\npet/alarmed: Haddad! HADDAD! Get a table!\n\n## case6_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'nightward'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\npet/stern: A pericardium full of embers. Eleven years, by that scar tissue. You've been walking around with this for eleven years, and you've been lecturing people about adequate.\nvoss/neutral: Eleven years, four months. I know, because I built its entrainment. The Foundation paid for my lab, and it was the finest lab I ever saw, and I was forty-seven and I wanted to be the first to make a thing that keeps time. I never asked what it was timing.\npet/alarmed: You made this.\nvoss/stern: I made its heart. And when I tried to stop, it was explained to me what would happen. I was shown a very small sample, and then I was shown a very small scar.\npet/stern: Give me one reason.\nvoss/soft: You'll need my notes. I can only dictate them if I'm breathing.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: Her temperature's doing something. It's going up in fives. Like, on purpose. It's counting.\nvoss/stern: If I die on your table, Perpetua, you'll have to fill out the form.\npet/soft: I have been looking forward to it.\n\n## case6_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'left'} ]\nvoss/soft: You stitch like a fishwife.\npet/soft: You bleed like a bank.\nvoss/neutral: Cordelia Vane. There. It's said. She has been funding the Introit, the Lacrimosa, all of it, since before her Foundation had a wing to put its name on. Every city she has built a hospital in, she has built an orchestra.\npet/stern: The lovely woman with the canapés.\nvoss/stern: She has never once been unkind to me. That is what you should be afraid of.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nteddy/soft: Um. Hi. Flowers came. For the whole theatre staff. From the Foundation. There's a really nice card about how much we're appreciated. Bashir signed for them. They smell amazing.\npet/alarmed: Teddy.\nteddy/alarmed: What?\npet/alarmed: What's the time signature of that card?\n\n## case7_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'alarm'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.8} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'right'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: He just went down. He was reading the card, and he said \"Doctor,\" and he went down. Bashir's on the floor. He's got everything. Pet says everything. All of them at once, in his chest, and the monitor's changing key.\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'centre'} ]\npet/stern: The flowers. Very nice. She sent them to the man who hands us our instruments.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: Where is he?\nteddy/alarmed: Mara! You're... you were in a coma! Sort of! You should be sitting!\nmara/stern: I heard him. Through two floors. He's a whole step flat, and he's never flat. Where is he?\npet/neutral: So you're the frightened child.\nmara/stern: Yes.\npet/soft: Good. Stay that way. It's the ones who stop that kill people.\n  [ {'camera': 'or'} ]\nnarrator/-: Theatre Three. The light comes on. For the first time in twenty-two years there is no one standing at the tray.\n  [ {'camera': 'paper'} ]\nmara/stern: Scalpel.\nnarrator/-: Nobody hands it to her.\nteddy/soft: ...I'll do it. I've seen it done. Mostly.\nmara/soft: Thank you, Teddy.\npet/stern: Well? Your hands, my dear. My hands. There's a man on that table who would have corrected both of us by now. Let's not keep him waiting.\n\n## case7_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'centre'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\nnarrator/-: It stopped the way a held breath stops. The monitor sat down on seventy-two, and stayed.\nbashir/soft: Clean, Doctor.\nmara/soft: You were flat. You were a whole step flat, Bashir, you frightened the life out of me.\nbashir/soft: I'll have it looked at.\npet/stern: You were dying for nine hours and you corrected my grip.\nbashir/neutral: It was a poor grip, Doctor.\npet/soft: ...Hm. I'm keeping this one.\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'farleft'} ]\nvoss/neutral: The Foundation has withdrawn its people from the wing, and from the city. They left the canapés.\nvoss/neutral: Dr. Quill. Dr. Nwosu. Mr. Haddad. Mr. Lark.\nvoss/soft: Adequate.\nteddy/soft: Did... did anybody else just get a chill?\n  [ {'exit': 'all'} ]\n  [ {'set': 'cabin'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'vane', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nnarrator/-: Somewhere past the breakwater, on a very good ship, a telephone is answered on the first ring.\nvane/soft: Yes. I heard. Wasn't she wonderful? That lovely ear.\nvane/soft: No, don't be cross. It was never going to end in Port Verity. A promising first movement. Have them tune the orchestra.\n132:[expr.neutral]\n141:[expr.alarmed]\n150:[expr.stern]\n159:[expr.soft]\n[[line]]\nset = \"scrub\"\n[[line]]\nmusic = \"tender\"\n[[line]]\nenter = \"mara\"\nat = \"left\"\n[[line]]\nenter = \"bashir\"\nat = \"right\"\n[[line]]\nwho = \"mara\"\n# In-op callouts, by trigger. `nurse` is whoever is calling the operation: Bashir, until he is the\n# patient. One line is picked at random each time.\n[wrong_tool]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Wrong instrument, Doctor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Not that one, Doctor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Doctor. The ot\n[healthy]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Doctor, that's healthy tissue.\"], [\"nurse\", \"That was healthy, Doctor. It was.\"]]\n[off_axis]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Along the barb, Doctor. Along it.\"], [\"nurse\", \"You tore it. Steady.\"]]\n[sloppy_suture]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"That stitch will leak, Doctor.\"]]\n[overheat]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Too long. You've burned him.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Let go sooner, Doctor.\"]]\n[overdose]\nlines = [[\"teddy\", \"Whoa, whoa, that's too much, that's a lot of drug, that's all the drug.\"]]\n[found]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"There. On the monitor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Found one. Eight seconds, Doctor.\"]]\n[immaculate]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Clean. Again.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Immaculate, Doctor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"...Doctor.\"], [\"teddy\", \"Oka\n[clean]\n50","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"092dc2e9-85ec-49f9-8a5e-82413bbc454c","uuid":"9696a21c-fbde-49e0-8a19-8c4fd99e61ed","timestamp":"2026-11-05T21:14:07.193Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"## case0_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.8} ]\nnarrator/-: Port Verity. Mercy Harbor General. The storm has been in the city for nine days, and the city has stopped expecting anything else.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: Second year. Four hundred and twelve nights in this building, and I can still tell you which radiator is flat by a semitone. It is the one outside Theatre Three. It has never once been fixed.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Doctor.\nmara/alarmed: Bashir. Where's Dr. Oyelaran? The board says there's a hand on the way up. A forearm. The board says \"attending.\"\nbashir/stern: The attending is elbow-deep in a bus driver. This one's yours, Doctor.\nmara/alarmed: Mine. As in... by myself. Solo. With the hands I have.\nbashir/neutral: Those are the ones I'd bring.\nmara/alarmed: I once dropped a retractor into a patient's lunch.\nbashir/soft: He enjoyed the lunch. A net-mender off Pier Nine, Doctor. A winch let go in the wind. He has hooks in him to the elbow and he is apologising to the porters.\nmara/soft: My father tuned pianos, Bashir. In this city, in this weather. He used to say a string doesn't care how frightened you are. It only cares where you put your hand.\nbashir/neutral: Then put it where I tell you. Scrub in. I'll hand you everything but the nerve.\n\n## case0_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/soft: Your hands were steady.\nmara/soft: They weren't. I was counting his heartbeats.\nbashir/neutral: Most people count sutures.\nmara/neutral: Seventy-two. He was seventy-two when we started. A little sharp around the second hook, then he came back. Did you hear it?\nbashir/neutral: I heard a monitor, Doctor.\nmara/soft: Yes. Of course. So did I.\nnarrator/-: Anton Deverell's hand opened and closed at 4:10 in the morning. He asked who to thank. Nobody on the night shift was willing to be named.\n\n## case1_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Harbour pilot, Doctor. Rosalind Pike. An appendix. There are first-years who could do this in the dark, and in this building they have.\nmara/neutral: Then why is the gallery lit?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\n  [ {'camera': 'or'} ]\nvoss/stern: Because I am in it, Dr. Quill.\n  [ {'camera': 'paper'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Chief Voss. I didn't... nobody said. It's a routine appendix, Chief.\nvoss/stern: In my hospital, adequate is a kind of malpractice. You closed a forearm last week, and the porters have been telling it like scripture. I came to see whether it was surgery or weather.\nmara/alarmed: Chief, I...\nvoss/neutral: Do not narrate, Doctor. Operate.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nteddy/neutral: Hey! I'm Teddy. I'm the one keeping her asleep, which I'm told is important. I do jokes when I'm scared, so if you hear jokes, that's information.\nbashir/neutral: He has never once stopped joking.\nteddy/soft: See? He gets it.\n\n## case1_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nmara/alarmed: An artery let go. On a routine appendix. On the routine appendix, with her watching. I had my hand in there, Bashir, and I felt it go before it went.\nbashir/neutral: You did.\nmara/neutral: What did she say? Did she say anything?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nvoss/stern: Adequate.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Was that... is that good?\nbashir/soft: Doctor, I have worked here twenty-two years. I have heard her say that twice. The other one has a wing named after him now.\n\n## case2_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 1} ]\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\nnarrator/-: At 2:40 in the morning, Grain Terminal Two stops being a building. They hear it at Mercy Harbor before they see it. The windows on the east side bow in, consider it, and hold.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'right'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: Okay! Okay okay okay. That's eleven. Eleven on trolleys, and they're lining them up by the lifts, and someone's asked me for a form, and I don't think we have that form!\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Who's worst?\nteddy/alarmed: Foreman. Gideon Marr. He went back in. For the lunch tins, Mara. For the night shift's lunch tins. He's got most of a conveyor in his chest.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nbashir/stern: There is no attending, Doctor. There is no theatre, either: we are setting up behind that curtain. This is a corridor with ambitions.\nmara/neutral: Then get me a scanner and get me a light. Teddy. Breathe.\nteddy/soft: I'm breathing. I'm the only one here who's supposed to be doing it for someone else, but I'm breathing.\n\n## case2_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: He'll live. All eleven of them will live. Someone should tell the terminal it didn't win.\nbashir/neutral: Yes, Doctor.\nmara/neutral: You keep looking at me.\nbashir/stern: At 3:06 you said \"now.\" Then the heart stopped. In that order.\nmara/alarmed: I...\nbashir/neutral: I have stood beside a great many surgeons. I don't have a question about it. I want you to know I am not asking.\nmara/soft: Thank you.\nbashir/soft: Eat something. I've left a sandwich on the autoclave. It may be sterile.\n\n## case3_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'atrium'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'vane', 'at': 'right'} ]\nvane/soft: To the night staff of Mercy Harbor. No, don't get up, don't you dare, you've been on your feet since the terminal. I only wanted to see the people who do it.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/alarmed: Mrs. Vane. It's... I'm only here for the canapés. I should be on the ward, I'm not really dressed for a wing.\nvane/soft: You must be Dr. Quill. Eleven men from Grain Terminal Two wrote to my Foundation about you. Handwritten. Do you know how rarely anyone handwrites a thing to money?\nmara/soft: I had a good nurse.\nvane/soft: You had good hands. I can always tell. My whole life I've been around people who make things keep time: clockmakers, conductors. You have a lovely ear, Doctor. I do hope you're looked after.\nnarrator/-: Cordelia Vane smiled, and a wing of the hospital smiled with her. It was the warmest thing that had happened to Mara Quill all year.\n  [ {'exit': 'vane'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Doctor. Courier from the Foundation, as it happens. Ines Calloway. A benign mass of the liver: elective, forty minutes, home for breakfast.\nmara/neutral: Benign.\nbashir/neutral: It says so on the film. In three places.\n\n## case3_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nmara/alarmed: It moved. You saw it. It split, and it walked, and it kept time, Bashir, it was keeping time with her heart!\nteddy/alarmed: I would like it noted that I said waltz. On the record. Is there a record?\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nvoss/stern: There is no record.\nvoss/stern: Theatre Three is sealed until morning. The film is lost. The specimen was benign. You will wash your hands, you will sign nothing, and you will tell no one what you did not see in this room.\nmara/alarmed: Chief. Time stopped. There was a... it went quiet, everything went quiet, and I could hear...\nvoss/neutral: Yes. I know what you could hear.\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\nteddy/soft: Did she just... did she say she knows?\nbashir/neutral: Wash your hands, Doctor.\n\n## case4_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'nightward'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nnarrator/-: Below the morgue level there is a lift the building's plans do not include. It smells of cold iron and old rain. It stops at a floor with no number.\nmara/alarmed: This isn't on the fire map.\nvoss/neutral: Very little of importance is. Mercy Harbor's founders built this theatre for cases that could not exist. We call it the Night Ward, because we are not a poetic profession.\nvoss/stern: We call it Requiem. Recombinant Enzymatic Quiescent Immuno-Evasive Microfilaria. It is the only organism ever designed to keep time.\nmara/alarmed: Keep time for what?\nvoss/stern: For the end of you, Doctor.\nvoss/neutral: It entrains to the host's heartbeat and it waits. Each strain plays in its own meter. Introit, three-four, you have met. There are others, named by someone with a sense of occasion.\nmara/alarmed: Someone made this?\nvoss/neutral: Tonight's patient you know. Rosalind Pike came back for a follow-up, and someone seeded her in recovery, in my hospital, on my floor. She is on the table. No guidance this time. There is nobody left who knows how, except, it would appear, you.\nmara/soft: I can't hear it from here. I could if I were closer.\nvoss/stern: Then go and be closer.\n\n## case4_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\nmara/soft: She's breathing. Sixty-eight. Lovely and flat. Chief, I can't seem to...\nmara/alarmed: I can't open my hands. They've locked. I can't feel... it's so quiet in here. Why is it so quiet?\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\n  [ {'exit': 'mara'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.5} ]\nnarrator/-: Dr. Mara Quill fell the way a piano falls: all at once, and with every string sounding.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/alarmed: Exhaustion. Her pressure's in the floor. She held the Hush for eleven minutes on no sleep. Chief, nobody's built for this.\nvoss/stern: No.\nvoss/neutral: There are three more strains, Mr. Haddad, and I have one surgeon, and she is on the floor. Get your coat.\nbashir/neutral: Where are we going, Chief?\nvoss/stern: To the bus depot on Cooper Street. We need a second opinion.\n\n## case5_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'depot'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\nnarrator/-: Three in the morning, the Cooper Street bus depot. The last route stopped running in the spring. The tea machine has not been told.\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\npet/stern: This tea is an insult. I've been coming here for it for six years. Don't sit there, young man, that's the bench I'm disappointed on.\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'left'} ]\nbashir/neutral: Dr. Nwosu. My name is Bashir Haddad. I was told you would say no.\npet/neutral: I say no to everyone. Look. No.\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'centre'} ]\npet/stern: Ah. Ottoline. I smelled the starch from the road.\nvoss/stern: Perpetua.\npet/stern: You've got old. It suits you. It's the first thing that has.\nvoss/neutral: I have a longshoreman with a shattered femur, and on his second film something is growing in the marrow, and it is keeping time.\npet/alarmed: ...In what meter?\nvoss/stern: That's why I'm here.\npet/neutral: I have buried better surgeons than you, and I liked them less. Look at this hand. Go on, look at it. It shakes. It has shaken since the Ostend ferry. I haven't held a scalpel in nine years.\nbashir/soft: I'll hold it for you, Doctor. Until you want it.\npet/soft: ...Hm. You're the one who says Doctor.\npet/stern: Scalpel. Oh, don't look so pleased. And somebody bring that machine's tea, I'm not done hating it.\n\n## case5_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'scrub'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'left'} ]\npet/soft: Nine years. And the hand, Ottoline. You saw the hand. For ten seconds I had the hands I had at thirty-three, and there were two of them on the outside of me, and they knew what they were doing.\nvoss/neutral: You always did have a flair for appearing twice.\npet/neutral: You knew it would come back. That's why you came for me and not for someone with a licence. How long have you known about this strain, Ottoline?\nvoss/stern: Longer than you have been disappointed in that tea.\npet/stern: Don't you be arch with me. You've gone a terrible colour. Ottoline. When did you last... Ottoline?\nvoss/alarmed: It's only that the fifth beat... the fifth beat is always so very\n  [ {'shake': 1} ]\n  [ {'exit': 'voss'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.6} ]\npet/alarmed: Haddad! HADDAD! Get a table!\n\n## case6_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'nightward'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\npet/stern: A pericardium full of embers. Eleven years, by that scar tissue. You've been walking around with this for eleven years, and you've been lecturing people about adequate.\nvoss/neutral: Eleven years, four months. I know, because I built its entrainment. The Foundation paid for my lab, and it was the finest lab I ever saw, and I was forty-seven and I wanted to be the first to make a thing that keeps time. I never asked what it was timing.\npet/alarmed: You made this.\nvoss/stern: I made its heart. And when I tried to stop, it was explained to me what would happen. I was shown a very small sample, and then I was shown a very small scar.\npet/stern: Give me one reason.\nvoss/soft: You'll need my notes. I can only dictate them if I'm breathing.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: Her temperature's doing something. It's going up in fives. Like, on purpose. It's counting.\nvoss/stern: If I die on your table, Perpetua, you'll have to fill out the form.\npet/soft: I have been looking forward to it.\n\n## case6_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'right'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'left'} ]\nvoss/soft: You stitch like a fishwife.\npet/soft: You bleed like a bank.\nvoss/neutral: Cordelia Vane. There. It's said. She has been funding the Introit, the Lacrimosa, all of it, since before her Foundation had a wing to put its name on. Every city she has built a hospital in, she has built an orchestra.\npet/stern: The lovely woman with the canapés.\nvoss/stern: She has never once been unkind to me. That is what you should be afraid of.\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'farright'} ]\nteddy/soft: Um. Hi. Flowers came. For the whole theatre staff. From the Foundation. There's a really nice card about how much we're appreciated. Bashir signed for them. They smell amazing.\npet/alarmed: Teddy.\nteddy/alarmed: What?\npet/alarmed: What's the time signature of that card?\n\n## case7_before.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'corridor'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'alarm'} ]\n  [ {'flash': 0.8} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'teddy', 'at': 'right'} ]\nteddy/alarmed: He just went down. He was reading the card, and he said \"Doctor,\" and he went down. Bashir's on the floor. He's got everything. Pet says everything. All of them at once, in his chest, and the monitor's changing key.\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'centre'} ]\npet/stern: The flowers. Very nice. She sent them to the man who hands us our instruments.\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\nmara/neutral: Where is he?\nteddy/alarmed: Mara! You're... you were in a coma! Sort of! You should be sitting!\nmara/stern: I heard him. Through two floors. He's a whole step flat, and he's never flat. Where is he?\npet/neutral: So you're the frightened child.\nmara/stern: Yes.\npet/soft: Good. Stay that way. It's the ones who stop that kill people.\n  [ {'camera': 'or'} ]\nnarrator/-: Theatre Three. The light comes on. For the first time in twenty-two years there is no one standing at the tray.\n  [ {'camera': 'paper'} ]\nmara/stern: Scalpel.\nnarrator/-: Nobody hands it to her.\nteddy/soft: ...I'll do it. I've seen it done. Mostly.\nmara/soft: Thank you, Teddy.\npet/stern: Well? Your hands, my dear. My hands. There's a man on that table who would have corrected both of us by now. Let's not keep him waiting.\n\n## case7_after.toml {}\n  [ {'set': 'recovery'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tender'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'bashir', 'at': 'centre'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'mara', 'at': 'left'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'pet', 'at': 'right'} ]\nnarrator/-: It stopped the way a held breath stops. The monitor sat down on seventy-two, and stayed.\nbashir/soft: Clean, Doctor.\nmara/soft: You were flat. You were a whole step flat, Bashir, you frightened the life out of me.\nbashir/soft: I'll have it looked at.\npet/stern: You were dying for nine hours and you corrected my grip.\nbashir/neutral: It was a poor grip, Doctor.\npet/soft: ...Hm. I'm keeping this one.\n  [ {'enter': 'voss', 'at': 'farleft'} ]\nvoss/neutral: The Foundation has withdrawn its people from the wing, and from the city. They left the canapés.\nvoss/neutral: Dr. Quill. Dr. Nwosu. Mr. Haddad. Mr. Lark.\nvoss/soft: Adequate.\nteddy/soft: Did... did anybody else just get a chill?\n  [ {'exit': 'all'} ]\n  [ {'set': 'cabin'} ]\n  [ {'music': 'tense'} ]\n  [ {'cue': 'thunder'} ]\n  [ {'enter': 'vane', 'at': 'centre'} ]\nnarrator/-: Somewhere past the breakwater, on a very good ship, a telephone is answered on the first ring.\nvane/soft: Yes. I heard. Wasn't she wonderful? That lovely ear.\nvane/soft: No, don't be cross. It was never going to end in Port Verity. A promising first movement. Have them tune the orchestra.\n132:[expr.neutral]\n141:[expr.alarmed]\n150:[expr.stern]\n159:[expr.soft]\n[[line]]\nset = \"scrub\"\n[[line]]\nmusic = \"tender\"\n[[line]]\nenter = \"mara\"\nat = \"left\"\n[[line]]\nenter = \"bashir\"\nat = \"right\"\n[[line]]\nwho = \"mara\"\n# In-op callouts, by trigger. `nurse` is whoever is calling the operation: Bashir, until he is the\n# patient. One line is picked at random each time.\n[wrong_tool]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Wrong instrument, Doctor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Not that one, Doctor.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Doctor. The ot\n[healthy]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Doctor, that's healthy tissue.\"], [\"nurse\", \"That was healthy, Doctor. It was.\"]]\n[off_axis]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Along the barb, Doctor. Along it.\"], [\"nurse\", \"You tore it. Steady.\"]]\n[sloppy_suture]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"That stitch will leak, Doctor.\"]]\n[overheat]\nlines = [[\"nurse\", \"Too long. You've burned him.\"], [\"nurse\", \"Let go sooner, Doctor.\"]]\n[overdose]\nlines = [[\"teddy\", \"Whoa, whoa, that's too much, t